<p>Indian soybean meal typically costs more than Brazilian, Argentine or US meal — Indian yields are lower and strong domestic crush demand for oil keeps export prices elevated. German feed buyers look at it anyway for one reason: India doesn't permit commercial cultivation of genetically modified soybean, so Indian meal is non-GMO by origin — not through a certification scheme, but structurally, because the crop grown in India isn't GM in the first place. For German and broader European feed programmes serving non-GMO-labelled meat, dairy and poultry supply chains, that's a characteristic price competition from South America can't replicate. This guide is for those buyers: how to specify the meal, what the German paperwork involves, how the price is built, and where first orders go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>On volume, the sources disagree. The Soybean Processors Association of India named Germany among India's top-five soybean-meal destinations for the OMY2024-25 marketing year, alongside France, Nepal, Bangladesh and Kenya, but gave no per-country figures to verify. The primary WITS/UN Comtrade calendar-2023 data in the table above records only a small German figure. Trade to Germany likely moves in large, infrequent shipments, and EU statistics sometimes record cargo against the Rotterdam or Hamburg gateway rather than the final buyer's country. Volume here is real but not evenly distributed, and the non-GMO reason to buy from India holds regardless of where Germany ranks by tonnage in any given year.</p>

<h2>Specification: get the protein grade right</h2>
<p>46% and 48% minimum crude protein are the two standard export grades, solvent extracted. German buyers, in our experience, specify tightly and expect a proper certificate of analysis — moisture, crude fibre, crude fat, sand/silica content and urease activity, which indicates whether heat treatment during processing was correctly calibrated. Have your specification precise before requesting a quote; vague specs slow the process down on this market more than most.</p>

<h2>Packing and container loading</h2>
<p>Standard packing is 25kg, 40kg or 50kg PP bags, or 1 MT jumbo bags (FIBC) for buyers with bulk intake handling — around 24-26 MT per 20ft container, loading being weight-limited rather than volume-limited. Transit to Hamburg runs 18-24 days; Bremerhaven is the secondary gateway some buyers prefer for onward inland logistics.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>German buyers new to sourcing soybean meal from India should expect the specification and documentation conversation to take somewhat longer than with less rigorous markets — not because anything is wrong, but because German feed buyers typically want the full certificate of analysis panel confirmed before committing, plus clarity on the non-GMO documentation chain of custody and your own TRACES NT entry-document registration. Build this into your first-order timeline expectations and treat it as a one-time setup cost: once specification and documentation are established, repeat orders move considerably faster.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't publish a fixed price for soybean meal, and any supplier who does without asking your specification first is quoting you against an average, not your order. Five things move the number: the global soy complex (Indian meal prices track world soybean and soymeal benchmarks even though the crop itself is grown domestically), the size and quality of that year's Indian soybean crop and how strong domestic crushers' oil demand is (both push export meal prices up when domestic demand is tight), the protein grade you specify (48% commands a premium over 46%), packaging format (bulk vessel loading is typically more freight-efficient per tonne than bagged container cargo, which shifts the landed-cost comparison), and prevailing INR/EUR/USD exchange rates plus current freight rates to Hamburg or Bremerhaven.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific protein grade, packaging, volume and destination port — not as a generic per-tonne rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: protein minimum (46% or 48%), moisture/urease tolerances if you have non-standard requirements, packaging preference (bagged or bulk), quantity, destination port, and your target delivery window. That's enough for us to come back with FOB/CIF pricing and a lead time.</p>
