<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>Most disputes on soybean meal shipments trace back to one of three things. The first is a protein-percentage shortfall against contract — this usually isn't fraud, it's a testing-methodology mismatch (dry-basis vs as-is basis protein readings differ, and if the contract doesn't specify which basis applies, both sides can be technically correct and still disagree). Always state the basis explicitly in your purchase order, not just the number.</p>
<p>The second is urease activity readings outside the expected 0.05-0.30 mg N/g/min range, which points to under- or over-heating during solvent extraction — under-processed meal retains anti-nutritional trypsin inhibitors that affect feed conversion, over-processed meal loses protein digestibility (browning/Maillard reaction). This is a genuine quality parameter, not a paperwork formality, and worth insisting on in every certificate of analysis rather than treating it as optional.</p>
<p>The third is moisture-related quality disputes on arrival, usually from inadequate container ventilation or moisture ingress during a long transit — soybean meal is hygroscopic, and 18-24 days at sea gives condensation cycles time to matter if bags weren't properly stacked and dunnage wasn't used. We ventilate and stow with this in mind as standard practice, and we'd recommend asking any supplier directly how they handle it rather than assuming it's automatic.</p>
<p>What first-time German buyers most commonly get wrong: treating the non-GMO declaration as a simple add-on document rather than understanding it needs to support your own downstream labelling claims — if your feed or food chain makes a non-GMO claim to consumers, your documentation chain of custody needs to hold up to the same scrutiny your buyer's auditors will apply. Ask what your specific labelling scheme requires before your first order, not after.</p>
